WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:00.367
I want to go

00:00:00.367 --> 00:00:00.934
now to the former

00:00:00.934 --> 00:00:02.802
assistant special Watergate prosecutor

00:00:02.802 --> 00:00:04.170
who turned down a chance

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:05.705
to represent Donald Trump in the Mar

00:00:05.705 --> 00:00:07.874
a Lago documents case.

00:00:07.874 --> 00:00:08.441
John Sale,

00:00:08.441 --> 00:00:09.809
I appreciate your time

00:00:09.809 --> 00:00:11.811
and welcome to the show.

00:00:11.811 --> 00:00:13.279
So I know that you know

00:00:13.279 --> 00:00:15.215
Robert Costello, right.

00:00:15.215 --> 00:00:17.017
So I gave a quick headline of him. Right.

00:00:17.017 --> 00:00:18.184
He has represented

00:00:18.184 --> 00:00:20.220
people like Steve Bannon, Rudy Giuliani.

00:00:20.253 --> 00:00:21.421
Some people will make judgments

00:00:21.421 --> 00:00:22.689
about that as they will.

00:00:22.689 --> 00:00:24.657
What can you tell us about him?

00:00:24.657 --> 00:00:25.492
What kind of a lawyer

00:00:25.492 --> 00:00:28.428
he is, his character?

00:00:28.561 --> 00:00:29.829
Well, I think we have to be careful

00:00:29.829 --> 00:00:32.665
about equating a lawyer with his clients.

00:00:32.899 --> 00:00:34.367
Whether that's a good thing

00:00:34.367 --> 00:00:35.335
or a bad thing.

00:00:35.335 --> 00:00:37.170
So I will.

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:39.506
Robert Costello, for starters, the former

00:00:39.506 --> 00:00:40.206
assistant U.S.

00:00:40.206 --> 00:00:41.007
attorney

00:00:41.007 --> 00:00:42.842
in the Southern District of New York,

00:00:42.842 --> 00:00:45.145
where I am also an alumni of that office.

00:00:45.145 --> 00:00:46.913
He has an excellent reputation.

00:00:46.913 --> 00:00:48.214
He did as a prosecutor.

00:00:48.214 --> 00:00:49.682
He does as a defense lawyer,

00:00:49.682 --> 00:00:50.750
is honorable.

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:52.052
He's a good lawyer.

00:00:52.052 --> 00:00:54.988
And I have no doubt zero that he would

00:00:55.955 --> 00:00:56.623
say double negative.

00:00:56.623 --> 00:00:58.458
He would not lie under oath.

00:00:58.458 --> 00:00:58.691
All right.

00:00:58.691 --> 00:01:00.493
Well, and

00:01:01.394 --> 00:01:03.496
so whatever he brings to the table,

00:01:03.496 --> 00:01:05.331
I don't think he has an agenda

00:01:05.331 --> 00:01:07.200
other than to tell the truth.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:07.500
All right.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:08.401
This is significant

00:01:08.401 --> 00:01:09.335
because obviously a lot

00:01:09.335 --> 00:01:11.037
could be resting on him.

00:01:11.037 --> 00:01:11.838
And, you know,

00:01:11.838 --> 00:01:12.639
he did come out

00:01:12.639 --> 00:01:14.507
after his 3 hours in front of the grand

00:01:14.507 --> 00:01:16.276
jury and spoke to the press.

00:01:16.276 --> 00:01:17.510
He said he was frustrated

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:18.411
with the experience

00:01:18.411 --> 00:01:19.646
because he said

00:01:19.646 --> 00:01:20.280
that he had wanted

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:21.781
to talk about 300 emails

00:01:21.781 --> 00:01:23.316
that he had exchanged with Michael Cohen

00:01:23.316 --> 00:01:24.918
that he thought were very important.

00:01:24.918 --> 00:01:25.785
But he said prosecutors

00:01:25.785 --> 00:01:26.986
did not want to talk about that.

00:01:26.986 --> 00:01:28.521
But they were way more narrow.

00:01:28.521 --> 00:01:31.324
And here's what he said about it.

00:01:31.991 --> 00:01:32.859
They were getting upset

00:01:32.859 --> 00:01:34.561
because they asked me a limited question

00:01:34.561 --> 00:01:36.362
based on one of these six emails,

00:01:36.362 --> 00:01:38.531
and I would volunteer information

00:01:38.698 --> 00:01:39.632
that I thought the grand

00:01:39.632 --> 00:01:40.834
jury needed to hear.

00:01:42.435 --> 00:01:43.703
So, John, what does this say to you?

00:01:43.703 --> 00:01:44.904
He would volunteer information

00:01:44.904 --> 00:01:46.106
to the grand jury needed to hear,

00:01:46.106 --> 00:01:47.941
but they would only ask about six emails

00:01:47.941 --> 00:01:49.042
when he had 300

00:01:49.042 --> 00:01:51.511
he thought were important.

00:01:51.511 --> 00:01:53.246
Well, where I start is that no

00:01:53.246 --> 00:01:54.214
one is above the law,

00:01:54.214 --> 00:01:55.648
including Donald Trump.

00:01:55.648 --> 00:01:56.950
But that has to be viewed

00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:58.451
in a spectrum of

00:01:58.451 --> 00:01:59.552
we don't want to be involved

00:01:59.552 --> 00:02:01.221
in selective prosecution.

00:02:01.221 --> 00:02:01.788
We don't want to be

00:02:01.788 --> 00:02:04.023
prosecuting Donald Trump for a matter.

00:02:04.023 --> 00:02:04.858
We wouldn't prosecute

00:02:04.858 --> 00:02:06.626
someone else for the

00:02:06.626 --> 00:02:08.161
I don't know why the prosecutor doesn't

00:02:08.161 --> 00:02:09.329
just let it all come out

00:02:09.329 --> 00:02:10.830
before the grand jury

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:13.366
and let them decide.

00:02:13.366 --> 00:02:15.368
A target of an investigation such as Mr.

00:02:15.368 --> 00:02:17.103
Trump has a right to have the grand

00:02:17.103 --> 00:02:19.139
jury hear exculpatory evidence

00:02:19.372 --> 00:02:21.407
and then let them be an independent body,

00:02:21.541 --> 00:02:25.111
weigh it all and and vote and apply

00:02:25.111 --> 00:02:26.746
the law equally to him

00:02:26.746 --> 00:02:28.381
the way they would to anybody else.

00:02:28.381 --> 00:02:29.816
So let me say that

00:02:29.816 --> 00:02:31.251
if you're going to compare

00:02:31.251 --> 00:02:33.052
the credibility of Michael Cohen

00:02:33.052 --> 00:02:34.487
and Bob Costello,

00:02:34.487 --> 00:02:34.988
I mean,

00:02:34.988 --> 00:02:37.157
that's not even worth a discussion.

00:02:37.157 --> 00:02:38.258
But, you know,

00:02:38.258 --> 00:02:39.359
Michael Cohen

00:02:39.359 --> 00:02:42.128
is a defense lawyers dream as a witness.

00:02:42.795 --> 00:02:43.696
I mean, a

00:02:44.063 --> 00:02:46.232
first year law student could destroy him

00:02:46.366 --> 00:02:47.734
on cross-examination.

00:02:47.734 --> 00:02:49.102
And there was not a camera

00:02:49.102 --> 00:02:50.170
he doesn't love.

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:52.272
He's on every TV show in the world.

00:02:52.272 --> 00:02:53.806
And I don't criticize the media

00:02:53.806 --> 00:02:55.508
or the public for wanting to hear it.

00:02:55.508 --> 00:02:56.943
But when I was a prosecutor

00:02:56.943 --> 00:02:58.344
or if I had a cooperator

00:02:58.344 --> 00:02:59.846
and they wanted every TV show,

00:02:59.846 --> 00:03:02.415
I would pull that cooperation deal.

00:03:02.415 --> 00:03:04.817
So let me just ask you the bottom line.

00:03:04.817 --> 00:03:06.286
As you see it, Ben,

00:03:06.286 --> 00:03:07.754
do you think there's any chance

00:03:07.754 --> 00:03:09.122
that Alvin Bragg does

00:03:09.122 --> 00:03:13.026
not indict Donald Trump at this point?

00:03:13.459 --> 00:03:15.495
I do think there is a chance

00:03:15.495 --> 00:03:16.529
if a prosecutor does

00:03:16.529 --> 00:03:18.164
a thorough investigation

00:03:18.164 --> 00:03:18.831
and decides

00:03:18.831 --> 00:03:19.966
not to prosecute,

00:03:19.966 --> 00:03:22.569
he has not failed in his obligation.

00:03:22.569 --> 00:03:24.737
And I think Bob Costello's testimony,

00:03:25.071 --> 00:03:27.307
if nothing else, should cause Mr.

00:03:27.307 --> 00:03:29.108
Bragg to at least pause

00:03:29.108 --> 00:03:31.177
and think if this is the right case,

00:03:31.544 --> 00:03:33.446
if there is the facts and the law,

00:03:33.446 --> 00:03:35.081
Donald Trump should be held accountable.

00:03:35.081 --> 00:03:35.648
But frankly,

00:03:35.648 --> 00:03:36.950
I don't think this is the case.

00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:39.085
And I think beyond a reasonable doubt

00:03:39.219 --> 00:03:40.053
that Bob Costello

00:03:40.053 --> 00:03:41.120
testimony tips

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:42.021
the scale in favor

00:03:42.021 --> 00:03:44.123
of not bringing this case.

00:03:44.123 --> 00:03:45.391
All right, John, thank you very much.

00:03:45.391 --> 00:03:47.727
I appreciate your time

00:03:47.727 --> 00:03:49.796
and the context that you bring to this,

00:03:49.796 --> 00:03:50.730
which is so important.

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:51.598
OUTFRONT now, Ryan

00:03:51.598 --> 00:03:53.466
Goodman, Karen Magnifico,

00:03:53.466 --> 00:03:55.535
Basil SMIKLE and Scott Jennings.

00:03:55.535 --> 00:03:56.736
Thanks very much to all of you.

00:03:56.736 --> 00:03:57.337
So, Karen,

00:03:57.337 --> 00:03:59.072
you worked in the Manhattan DA's office,

00:03:59.072 --> 00:03:59.939
so you're very familiar

00:03:59.939 --> 00:04:00.807
with all of this, right?

00:04:00.807 --> 00:04:02.041
You know, obviously,

00:04:02.041 --> 00:04:02.508
you were there

00:04:02.508 --> 00:04:03.243
when there was a decision

00:04:03.243 --> 00:04:04.377
not to go ahead and indict

00:04:04.377 --> 00:04:07.313
on other issues related to Donald Trump.

00:04:07.747 --> 00:04:08.881
How much do you think

00:04:08.881 --> 00:04:11.184
Costello could impact the decision

00:04:11.184 --> 00:04:13.119
on whether or not to indict Trump?

00:04:13.119 --> 00:04:14.821
I think at this point

00:04:14.821 --> 00:04:16.022
that Alvin Bragg

00:04:16.022 --> 00:04:18.658
will likely ask the grand jury to decide.

00:04:18.658 --> 00:04:18.925
Right.

00:04:18.925 --> 00:04:20.126
They've heard all the evidence

00:04:20.126 --> 00:04:21.928
and they've also heard COSTELLO. Right.

00:04:21.928 --> 00:04:23.162
He was there for over three

00:04:23.162 --> 00:04:24.631
or almost 3 hours today.

00:04:24.631 --> 00:04:26.132
So to say that

00:04:26.132 --> 00:04:27.567
there was any selective

00:04:27.567 --> 00:04:28.568
questioning of him,

00:04:28.568 --> 00:04:30.169
he had almost 3 hours to say

00:04:30.169 --> 00:04:31.971
whatever it is that he wanted to say.

00:04:31.971 --> 00:04:32.372
The grand

00:04:32.372 --> 00:04:34.440
jury will have heard his testimony.

00:04:34.741 --> 00:04:36.175
And I think at this point, Alvin

00:04:36.175 --> 00:04:37.377
Bragg will likely

00:04:37.377 --> 00:04:39.412
leave it up to the grand jury to decide

00:04:39.412 --> 00:04:40.546
if they believe that

00:04:40.546 --> 00:04:42.448
this case beyond a reasonable doubt.

00:04:42.448 --> 00:04:43.216
That's not the standard

00:04:43.216 --> 00:04:44.217
in the grand jury, by the way.

00:04:44.217 --> 00:04:44.984
The standard in the grand

00:04:44.984 --> 00:04:46.653
jury is probable cause. Right.

00:04:46.653 --> 00:04:48.421
But I am sure simple majority. Right.

00:04:48.421 --> 00:04:49.822
So it's exactly like an indictment.

00:04:49.822 --> 00:04:51.124
You get one pretty much.

00:04:51.124 --> 00:04:51.691
But, you know,

00:04:51.691 --> 00:04:52.558
sometimes when

00:04:52.558 --> 00:04:54.460
the defense presents evidence,

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:55.561
whether it's the defendant

00:04:55.561 --> 00:04:57.130
testifying or other evidence

00:04:57.130 --> 00:04:58.831
that's exculpatory and again,

00:04:58.831 --> 00:04:59.899
almost 3 hours,

00:04:59.899 --> 00:05:01.301
that's a long time to say

00:05:01.301 --> 00:05:03.369
whatever it is he had to say.

00:05:03.369 --> 00:05:04.771
And I think that that

00:05:04.771 --> 00:05:07.273
that could influence a grand jury here.

00:05:07.273 --> 00:05:08.508
So we'll see.

00:05:08.508 --> 00:05:09.942
But I don't think that Alvin Bragg

00:05:09.942 --> 00:05:11.678
will not ask the grand jury

00:05:11.678 --> 00:05:13.913
to vote on this matter at this point.

00:05:13.946 --> 00:05:14.447
Okay.

00:05:14.447 --> 00:05:15.748
And Ryan,

00:05:15.748 --> 00:05:17.283
so Michael Cohen

00:05:17.283 --> 00:05:18.518
was not asked to testify.

00:05:18.518 --> 00:05:20.920
He was there to be prepared for rebuttal,

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:22.221
Costello testified.

00:05:22.221 --> 00:05:23.823
And then they said they did not need

00:05:23.823 --> 00:05:24.490
Michael Cohen,

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:26.192
which we'll see if that's significant.

00:05:26.192 --> 00:05:27.360
But then Cohen

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:28.127
obviously went out

00:05:28.127 --> 00:05:30.196
and gave a television review,

00:05:30.196 --> 00:05:32.832
and here's what he said.

00:05:33.866 --> 00:05:35.535
Typical Donald J.

00:05:35.535 --> 00:05:38.004
Trump play out of the playbook.

00:05:38.404 --> 00:05:39.872
Figure out how you're going to muddy

00:05:39.872 --> 00:05:41.841
the water as best as you possibly can.

00:05:41.841 --> 00:05:44.143
Denigrate the person, disparage them.

00:05:44.344 --> 00:05:45.845
They did the same thing to Cassidy

00:05:45.845 --> 00:05:46.446
Hutchinson.

00:05:46.446 --> 00:05:47.580
They did the same thing

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:51.217
to anyone and everyone that is it

00:05:51.784 --> 00:05:55.021
is for the truth.

00:05:55.621 --> 00:05:56.823
So he goes on to say,

00:05:56.823 --> 00:05:58.558
The beauty is that I have facts.

00:05:58.558 --> 00:05:59.158
Okay.

00:05:59.158 --> 00:05:59.959
Now you

00:05:59.959 --> 00:06:01.060
obviously hear what John Sayles says

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:02.128
about Costello

00:06:02.128 --> 00:06:03.529
and his unimpeachable integrity

00:06:03.529 --> 00:06:05.565
and the way, you know, his reputation.

00:06:05.565 --> 00:06:07.867
But but what does this come down to then

00:06:07.867 --> 00:06:10.303
for for a grand jury?

00:06:10.303 --> 00:06:12.872
So I think in part, it might come down to

00:06:12.872 --> 00:06:13.940
Cohen testifying.

00:06:13.940 --> 00:06:16.109
But then what is there to corroborate

00:06:16.109 --> 00:06:17.610
what he has to say?

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:18.745
Prosecutors often

00:06:18.745 --> 00:06:20.680
have to include accomplices to the crime

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:21.781
or some bad actors,

00:06:21.781 --> 00:06:23.383
especially when they're prosecuting,

00:06:23.383 --> 00:06:24.650
for example, organized crime.

00:06:24.650 --> 00:06:25.251
So you're going to have

00:06:25.251 --> 00:06:27.387
some prosecutors, witnesses like that,

00:06:27.420 --> 00:06:29.422
who have flipped and are cooperating

00:06:29.422 --> 00:06:29.722
that aren't

00:06:29.722 --> 00:06:30.890
the most appealing characters

00:06:30.890 --> 00:06:32.258
and have done things like lie in the past

00:06:32.258 --> 00:06:33.493
and commit past crimes.

00:06:33.493 --> 00:06:34.060
But you need to

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:34.627
then go through

00:06:34.627 --> 00:06:36.229
and corroborate everything.

00:06:36.229 --> 00:06:37.597
So there is corroboration

00:06:37.597 --> 00:06:39.599
in the case in the form of checks

00:06:39.599 --> 00:06:40.066
that are written

00:06:40.066 --> 00:06:41.167
by Donald Trump,

00:06:41.167 --> 00:06:41.667
an audio

00:06:41.667 --> 00:06:43.269
recording with Donald Trump

00:06:43.269 --> 00:06:44.604
and Michael Cohen

00:06:44.604 --> 00:06:45.538
and other witnesses

00:06:45.538 --> 00:06:46.539
that can testify as well,

00:06:46.539 --> 00:06:47.707
because part of this involved

00:06:47.707 --> 00:06:48.608
the National Enquirer

00:06:48.608 --> 00:06:50.510
as part of the hush money payment scheme,

00:06:50.510 --> 00:06:52.545
and they have even a public document

00:06:52.545 --> 00:06:54.480
with the federal prosecutors

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:56.783
that explains their role in the scheme

00:06:56.949 --> 00:06:57.617
and that it was done

00:06:57.617 --> 00:06:58.484
to influence the election.

00:06:58.484 --> 00:07:00.286
So there's a lot of corroboration.

00:07:00.286 --> 00:07:00.887
It's a question

00:07:00.887 --> 00:07:02.188
of whether that can be put together

00:07:02.188 --> 00:07:03.256
in such a way

00:07:03.256 --> 00:07:04.690
that it would convince a jury.

00:07:04.690 --> 00:07:06.025
And that is an open question,

00:07:06.025 --> 00:07:07.126
and that is the open question,

00:07:07.126 --> 00:07:09.061
because you also have, of course,

00:07:09.061 --> 00:07:10.096
you know, a D.A.

00:07:10.096 --> 00:07:12.331
here. Right? It's an elected position.

00:07:12.331 --> 00:07:13.299
There's a lot at stake. Right.

00:07:13.299 --> 00:07:14.534
You want reelection?

00:07:14.534 --> 00:07:15.001
You know,

00:07:15.334 --> 00:07:16.202
I would assume DA's

00:07:16.202 --> 00:07:16.769
let's just assume

00:07:16.769 --> 00:07:17.870
they always want to do the right thing.

00:07:17.870 --> 00:07:19.038
But, you know, there's it's

00:07:19.038 --> 00:07:19.739
there's pressure

00:07:20.740 --> 00:07:22.141
after everything that the

00:07:22.141 --> 00:07:24.043
D.A., Alvin Bragg, has gone through here,

00:07:24.043 --> 00:07:25.545
is there an off ramp for him

00:07:25.545 --> 00:07:26.279
if he wants one?

00:07:26.279 --> 00:07:27.146
I mean, you heard Karen say he's

00:07:27.146 --> 00:07:27.713
going to go ahead

00:07:27.713 --> 00:07:30.149
and hand this to the grand jury.

00:07:30.149 --> 00:07:32.251
Is there an off ramp if he wants one?

00:07:32.518 --> 00:07:34.086
He is in an incredibly

00:07:34.086 --> 00:07:35.288
difficult position.

00:07:35.288 --> 00:07:37.356
You know, here's a guy who and I know him

00:07:37.390 --> 00:07:38.791
voted for him.

00:07:39.325 --> 00:07:40.259
If you look at his background,

00:07:40.259 --> 00:07:42.261
he has represented white collar

00:07:42.462 --> 00:07:43.696
while he's been involved

00:07:43.696 --> 00:07:45.298
in white collar crimes

00:07:45.298 --> 00:07:47.366
or fraud investigations.

00:07:47.700 --> 00:07:48.801
He was worked at the U.S.

00:07:48.801 --> 00:07:49.469
attorney's office.

00:07:49.469 --> 00:07:51.304
So he sort of understands how that works.

00:07:51.304 --> 00:07:54.040
And despite what may happen in New York

00:07:54.040 --> 00:07:55.741
or maybe even counterintuitive

00:07:55.741 --> 00:07:58.010
to being an elected official in New York,

00:07:58.010 --> 00:07:58.578
he's going to

00:07:58.578 --> 00:07:59.946
try to stay out of the papers

00:07:59.946 --> 00:08:01.113
and off of TV

00:08:01.113 --> 00:08:03.216
because he wants to be very careful.

00:08:03.216 --> 00:08:04.050
On the other hand,

00:08:04.050 --> 00:08:04.650
he campaigned

00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:06.252
as a criminal justice reform

00:08:06.252 --> 00:08:07.119
and as such,

00:08:07.119 --> 00:08:08.821
he has been pilloried from the right,

00:08:08.821 --> 00:08:09.922
particularly Donald Trump.

00:08:09.922 --> 00:08:10.423
Lee Zeldin,

00:08:10.423 --> 00:08:11.891
who ran for governor, ran

00:08:11.891 --> 00:08:13.860
on taking him out of office

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:15.294
if he had won.

00:08:15.294 --> 00:08:18.297
So he's an incredibly difficult position

00:08:18.297 --> 00:08:21.334
because if he chooses, not if there is no

00:08:21.334 --> 00:08:22.301
indictment here,

00:08:23.402 --> 00:08:23.970
Donald Trump

00:08:23.970 --> 00:08:25.171
can say, look, my protest,

00:08:25.171 --> 00:08:26.439
my supporters got out there,

00:08:26.439 --> 00:08:27.707
they pushed him back.

00:08:27.707 --> 00:08:29.108
And they're going to be questions

00:08:29.108 --> 00:08:29.909
about whether or not

00:08:29.909 --> 00:08:31.244
he was up to the task.

00:08:31.244 --> 00:08:31.978
On the other hand,

00:08:31.978 --> 00:08:34.113
if there is an indictment,

00:08:34.113 --> 00:08:34.614
Donald Trump

00:08:34.614 --> 00:08:35.581
is going to come out and say,

00:08:35.581 --> 00:08:37.850
look at this Democratic zealot

00:08:37.850 --> 00:08:40.052
going after going, going after us.

00:08:40.052 --> 00:08:40.953
He won't even say me.

00:08:40.953 --> 00:08:42.455
He'll say us. Right.

00:08:42.455 --> 00:08:43.322
So it's

00:08:43.322 --> 00:08:45.291
a tough position for him to be in.

00:08:45.291 --> 00:08:47.193
But I have to think that he

00:08:47.193 --> 00:08:49.295
has enough experience

00:08:49.295 --> 00:08:51.631
and support to at least be

00:08:52.798 --> 00:08:53.933
I want to say hold back,

00:08:53.933 --> 00:08:55.568
but at least be a lot more careful

00:08:55.568 --> 00:08:57.403
than maybe one would think.

00:08:57.403 --> 00:08:57.937
He's the former

00:08:57.937 --> 00:08:59.238
president of the United States.

00:08:59.238 --> 00:08:59.972
Tough call.

00:08:59.972 --> 00:09:00.239
You know,

00:09:00.239 --> 00:09:01.941
you said something in your answer that

00:09:01.941 --> 00:09:03.509
that caught my attention.

00:09:03.509 --> 00:09:05.444
You said if he doesn't indict,

00:09:05.444 --> 00:09:07.880
people will say he wasn't up to the task.

00:09:08.214 --> 00:09:09.115
What task?

00:09:09.115 --> 00:09:09.949
What's the task?

00:09:09.949 --> 00:09:10.750
Is the task

00:09:10.750 --> 00:09:13.019
to get Donald Trump at all costs

00:09:13.019 --> 00:09:14.253
or is the task

00:09:14.253 --> 00:09:14.520
to do

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:17.890
what's right or to just execute the laws?

00:09:17.890 --> 00:09:19.659
And I think that's where Republicans

00:09:19.659 --> 00:09:21.260
have legitimate questions

00:09:21.260 --> 00:09:21.661
about this

00:09:21.661 --> 00:09:23.563
prosecutor, is he's thinking about

00:09:23.563 --> 00:09:24.897
his political constituency.

00:09:24.897 --> 00:09:26.332
Who would say what you just said?

00:09:26.332 --> 00:09:27.099
If you don't get him,

00:09:27.099 --> 00:09:28.200
you weren't up to the task

00:09:28.200 --> 00:09:30.336
or is he actually thinking about the law?

00:09:30.369 --> 00:09:31.971
You know, as prosecutor,

00:09:31.971 --> 00:09:33.072
he has downgraded something

00:09:33.072 --> 00:09:35.174
like 52% of felonies

00:09:35.174 --> 00:09:36.042
down to misdemeanors.

00:09:36.042 --> 00:09:37.510
In this case, he's taken a seven year

00:09:37.510 --> 00:09:38.744
old paperwork misdemeanor

00:09:38.744 --> 00:09:41.881
and inflating it to a convoluted novel

00:09:42.782 --> 00:09:43.416
felony.

00:09:43.416 --> 00:09:44.850
And I think a lot of Republicans

00:09:44.850 --> 00:09:46.719
are looking at him saying,

00:09:46.719 --> 00:09:47.520
thinking,

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:48.921
is he more political

00:09:48.921 --> 00:09:50.690
than justice minded here?

00:09:50.690 --> 00:09:52.391
Because of where he represents

00:09:52.391 --> 00:09:53.359
in the voters that he has?

00:09:53.359 --> 00:09:54.493
Since you asked.

00:09:54.493 --> 00:09:57.196
I would say for the last several years,

00:09:57.363 --> 00:09:58.331
through two impeachment

00:09:58.331 --> 00:10:00.633
hearings, through a January six hearing,

00:10:00.633 --> 00:10:02.001
there's a constant

00:10:02.001 --> 00:10:03.869
push to hold him accountable.

00:10:03.869 --> 00:10:04.704
So the question is,

00:10:04.704 --> 00:10:06.606
is this holding him accountable?

00:10:06.606 --> 00:10:08.641
If if the facts say that

00:10:08.641 --> 00:10:09.542
we need to move on

00:10:09.542 --> 00:10:10.710
and then look at something else?

00:10:10.710 --> 00:10:11.310
I get that

00:10:11.310 --> 00:10:13.312
I think most both voters would get that.

00:10:13.579 --> 00:10:14.947
But there is going to also

00:10:14.947 --> 00:10:16.415
be this question of

00:10:16.415 --> 00:10:17.316
is there going to ever

00:10:17.316 --> 00:10:18.050
come a point in time

00:10:18.050 --> 00:10:18.584
when Donald Trump

00:10:18.584 --> 00:10:19.452
will be held accountable

00:10:19.452 --> 00:10:20.252
for the things that he's.

00:10:20.252 --> 00:10:21.320
Well, I would think

00:10:21.387 --> 00:10:21.587
I mean,

00:10:21.587 --> 00:10:22.788
I would think the people in Georgia

00:10:22.788 --> 00:10:25.057
and the special counsel at DOJ

00:10:25.057 --> 00:10:27.093
have more legitimate things to care about

00:10:27.093 --> 00:10:27.593
than perhaps.

00:10:27.593 --> 00:10:28.461
And it's interesting

00:10:28.461 --> 00:10:28.995
it will be interesting

00:10:28.995 --> 00:10:29.462
to know

00:10:29.462 --> 00:10:30.596
what they think about this

00:10:30.596 --> 00:10:31.163
and whether, you know,

00:10:31.163 --> 00:10:32.765
if they also were taken aback,

00:10:32.765 --> 00:10:33.633
as everyone

00:10:33.633 --> 00:10:34.200
was, frankly,

00:10:34.200 --> 00:10:35.401
by the fact that this appears

00:10:35.401 --> 00:10:36.902
to be coming first before anything else.

